**Rollout**: 10%, split evenly. 5% of identities see PayPal, 5% act as control, and the remaining 90% stay out of the experiment entirely. - **Primary metric**: Checkout Completion, expected to **increase**.

Launch it from the Review step.

On the checkout page, resolve the flag (recording the exposure) and branch on the variation name. When a checkout
completes, send the conversion event. The SDK must be initialised with `enableEvents: true` and the user identified; see
Run an Experiment.

```tsx
import flagsmith from '@flagsmith/flagsmith';
import { useExperiment } from '@flagsmith/flagsmith/react';

const CheckoutPage: React.FC = () => {
 // Evaluates the flag and records the exposure
 const flag = useExperiment('paypal_button');
 const showPaypal = flag?.enabled && flag?.variant === 'show-paypal';

 const onOrderConfirmed = () => {
  // Record the conversion
  flagsmith.trackEvent('checkout_completed');
 };

 return (
  <>
   <CardPaymentForm onConfirmed={onOrderConfirmed} />
   {showPaypal ? <PayPalButton onConfirmed={onOrderConfirmed} /> : null}
  </>
 );
};
```

Outside React, `flagsmith.getExperimentFlag('paypal_button')` does the same job as the hook. The same flow works in the
other supported SDKs; see Run an Experiment.

Self-Hosting and Open Source. The SDKs buffer events and send them in batches, by default every 10 seconds. Send what is left before your application exits, as shown in each tab.

```javascript
import flagsmith from '@flagsmith/flagsmith';

await flagsmith.init({
    environmentID: 'YOUR_CLIENT_SIDE_ENVIRONMENT_KEY',
    enableEvents: true,
    // Self-hosted only:
    // api: 'https://flagsmith.example.com/api/v1/',
    // eventProcessorConfig: { eventsApiUrl: 'https://events.flagsmith.example.com/' },
});
await flagsmith.identify('user_42');

// Before a server-side render responds, or before the page unloads
await flagsmith.flushEvents();
```

`eventProcessorConfig` requires `enableEvents: true`; `init` throws without it.

```jsx
